---
import GravityEncounter from "../../../components/studies/gravity/GravityEncounter.svelte";
import PlanetEncounter from "../../../components/studies/gravity/PlanetEncounter.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "gravity-wells");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="situation">
		<h2 id="situation">01 / The close pass should be a temptation</h2>
		<p>
			Imagine approaching a sun with room to pass safely outside it. Cutting
			closer should offer an exciting alternative: bend the ship’s path, gather
			speed, and escape with a useful advantage. Cut too close, and that same
			pull can make escape difficult or impossible before heat destroys the
			ship. A planet poses the same temptation, with impact replacing solar
			burn.
		</p>
		<p>
			The central tension is a rewarding pass beside a dangerous commitment. A
			well’s reach, inward growth of pull, and visible cues should help players
			learn that distinction. Escape also depends on approach speed, direction,
			and the ship’s thrust; a drawn ring cannot universally promise safety.
			Shared rules should explain differences between bodies without making
			every encounter identical.
		</p>
		<p>
			There is a useful physics distinction: an unpowered pass around a
			stationary body gains speed on approach and gives it back on departure at
			equal distance. Real gravity assists exchange energy with a moving body (<a
				href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/gravity-assists/"
				>NASA</a
			>). For the desired solar slingshot, we therefore need to investigate what
			useful speed the player carries away, alongside the thrill of accelerating
			nearby. That experience should guide the eventual size and force rules.
		</p>
		<p>
			We want useful close passes, readable danger, and shared rules for suns
			and planets. The comparisons below establish the force curve and an
			initial strength range; physical sizing stays outside this study.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="carry-away">
		<h2 id="carry-away">02 / What does the ship carry away?</h2>
		<p>
			A bend in the trail can look like a slingshot even when the ship leaves
			slower. This first comparison isolates the original stationary sun against
			the same launch without a sun. The hypothesis is that inward acceleration
			alone may not deliver the useful exit advantage we want.
		</p>
		<p>
			Start with the default pass, then reduce the aim offset to approach the
			center more closely. Compare peak and exit speed; scrub the replay to see
			where acceleration becomes a difficult departure. Change entry speed to
			see why distance alone cannot define the risk.
		</p>
		<h3>02.1 / Coasting with and without resistance</h3>
		<p>
			The demo now removes passive drag and the speed cap. Toggle the original
			baseline at the same speed and aim to compare. Engines remain off in both
			cases: this tests momentum preservation, not the directional engine
			response.
		</p>
		<GravityEncounter client:load />
		<p>
			Original baseline (checkbox enabled): at entry speed 500 and aim offset
			80: the solar pass peaks at 539, then exits at 317 units/s; the no-sun
			reference exits at 346. The nearby acceleration is real, but this pass
			does not retain a speed reward. A direct approach at offset 0 ends in sun
			contact. These are observations of this fixture, not a verdict on gameplay
			feel.
		</p>
		<p>
			This diagnoses the existing rule rather than choosing a replacement. A
			faster exit than the no-sun reference can still be slower than entry. A
			powered escape and a moving planet need separate comparisons before we
			generalize this result to all wells.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Review: interesting loops at low speed, too little influence at useful
				flight speeds.</strong
			>
			We found appealing loops at low speeds, but little visible influence at an
			entry speed of 325 with the original tuning. The aim offset was not
			recorded, so this is an experience judgment, not a universal numerical
			threshold. It identifies a prerequisite for the slingshot: the well must
			meaningfully bend a ship moving at gameplay speeds. Retained exit speed
			remains unresolved.
		</p>
		<p>
			A faster ship spends less time under the pull and needs more acceleration
			to bend through the same radius. Simply increasing strength could also
			make the appealing slow loops lethal. Comparing trajectory influence
			across entry speeds remains useful, but the propulsion decision below now
			takes priority over tuning well strength, reach, and curve shape.
		</p>
		<h3>Engines limit thrust, not momentum</h3>
		<p>
			Each of our three ships has two engines. Their effectiveness depends on
			push direction relative to motion, replacing passive drag. Gravity-earned
			speed should not be clipped by an arbitrary speed cap. Demo 02.1 preserves
			the original resistance model for comparison; section 03 describes the
			engine response now used in the game.
		</p>
		<p>
			Removing a cap lets gravity change speed without artificial clipping. It
			does not make a stationary well a source of unlimited net energy. We still
			need effective steering and braking at high speed; the initial engine
			estimate is carried into the alpha for later playtesting.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="curve-study">
		<h2 id="curve-study">02.2 / A steep center and a quiet edge</h2>
		<p>
			We use inverse-square falloff with zero pull at the border. Pure
			inverse-square gravity has no finite border. This candidate uses a(r) = μ
			× (1/r² − 1/R²) inside radius R, and zero outside. Distance r is measured
			from the body’s center. This is a game adaptation: continuous at the
			border, though its slope changes there.
		</p>
		<p>
			The comparison matches both curves at half the existing well radius,
			keeping reach and the body unchanged. That reference is an experimental
			control, not an accepted strength rule. The surface ends a pass before the
			formula’s central singularity. Try the same entry speed and aim with each
			curve; inspect the tighter inner turn and the quieter outer approach.
		</p>
		<p>
			The new distance-dependent curves give us the shapes we want, but the
			original strength leaves the close-in pull too weak. Strength is now
			adjustable from 0.25× to 8× without changing the body or well radius. Keep
			entry speed and aim fixed while adjusting it.
		</p>
		<GravityEncounter
			label="Gravity curve comparison"
			curveStudy={true}
			client:load
		/>
		<p>
			We chose the bounded inverse-square curve and a solar strength range of
			4×–10× because it produces the close-pass shapes we want. The comparison
			slider reaches 8×, so 8×–10× remains unverified here. The game starts at
			4×; section 03 records the initial settings.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="planet-study">
		<h2 id="planet-study">02.3 / A small planet, a moving well</h2>
		<p>
			Physical size is out of scope: this trial fixes a small rock planet at
			radius 20. Strength and reach are independent controls, with no
			proportional relationship to that radius. The sun result stays preserved
			above.
		</p>
		<p>
			Compare the same launch around a stationary planet and an orbiting one.
			First keep strength and reach fixed and reverse orbital direction: does
			the encounter give a useful speed gain, or take speed away? Then tune the
			well. The hypothesis is that planetary motion can make the slingshot
			reward depend on approach side.
		</p>
		<PlanetEncounter client:load />
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Which approaches produce the useful turn and exit-speed reward you want
				from a planet?</strong
			>
			Physical size stays fixed while we investigate strength, reach, and
			motion.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="next">
		<h2 id="next">03 / What we carry into the game</h2>
		<p>
			Use bounded inverse-square gravity for suns and planets, with zero pull at
			the well boundary. Remove passive drag and the hard speed cap, and make
			engine effectiveness depend on push direction. The comparisons show why
			these changes belong together: resistance was masking the well's influence
			and removing speed gained during a pass.
		</p>
		<p>
			Keep existing body sizes and well reaches for this integration. Planet
			strength and reach are independent of physical size. New sizing rules,
			refined planet ranges, and visual danger cues can wait; they are not
			needed to bring this improvement into the game.
		</p>
		<h3>What the study establishes</h3>
		<p>
			The preserved coasting and curve comparisons support the gravity model and
			the 4×–10× solar direction. They show trajectory behavior, not how the
			combined game feels to play. Engine effectiveness and planet strength
			remain initial estimates. Automated checks cover steering, braking,
			contact, and escape in the shared simulation.
		</p>
		<p>
			Playtesting happens after integration, on the built alpha. That is where
			we will judge control, difficulty, and the reward of a close pass, then
			revisit the provisional engine and strength settings. It is outside this
			study and is not a prerequisite for integration.
		</p>
		<h3>Implemented in the shared flight simulation</h3>
		<p>
			The game and <a href="/lab/flight">Flight Lab</a> now use bounded
			inverse-square wells, no passive drag or speed cap, and two directional
			engines per ship. Initial estimates are 4× solar strength, planetary
			acceleration 300 units/s² at half reach, and engine half-effectiveness at
			600 units/s along the push. Opposing and perpendicular thrust remain fully
			effective. Existing sizes and reaches are preserved.
		</p>
		<p>
			Four physics substeps per replay tick resolve close passes. Automated
			checks cover coasting, braking and outward escape across all three ships,
			differential steering, contact, and deterministic replay. New scores are
			isolated in the gravity-1 challenge. Score-rule and two-player leaderboard
			checks also pass for release 0.3.4; the updated rules accompany the game.
			The local build is available for the later alpha playtest; no human
			playtest result is claimed.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
.invitation {
	border-left: var(--flight-border);
	border-left-color: var(--color-acid-lime);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
